<h2 id="choose-purpose">Choose the decision and bring the right inputs</h2>
<p>For a buyer scoping meeting, the decision is whether a defined training need and a workable delivery plan exist. Invite the sales or enablement owner who can describe the current workflow and approve a next step. Bring one approved, anonymized example of the behavior being discussed, or use a fictional scenario if real examples are unavailable.</p>
<p>For a rep practice session, the decision is which behavior each learner will practice next and how the manager will review it. Prepare a shared role-play, a short observation rubric, and a way to capture feedback. Neither meeting needs private customer records, an unsupported savings estimate, or a long generic capabilities presentation.</p>
<h2 id="scoping-agenda">A 30-minute buyer scoping agenda</h2>
<div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Thirty-minute sales training scoping agenda" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Output: an agreed training brief or a documented reason to pause</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Minutes</th><th scope="col">Owner and activity</th><th scope="col">Output</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">0–5 · 5 minutes</th><td>Buyer and provider confirm the purpose, participants, and decision this meeting should support.</td><td>A shared scope question.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">5–12 · 7 minutes</th><td>Buyer describes one current sales behavior and the evidence available to review it.</td><td>A baseline example, with unknowns identified.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">12–20 · 8 minutes</th><td>Both sides choose one practice behavior and agree what a reviewer could observe.</td><td>A draft practice objective and observation criteria.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">20–26 · 6 minutes</th><td>Delivery owners review participant availability, format, access needs, manager capacity, and preparation.</td><td>Named dependencies and owners.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">26–30 · 4 minutes</th><td>Buyer and provider agree the next decision, required evidence, owner, and date—or stop.</td><td>An owned next action or a no-fit decision.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The durations reconcile: 5 + 7 + 8 + 6 + 4 = 30 minutes. If the buyer cannot identify a relevant behavior or available participants, record that finding. Do not convert an exploratory conversation into an approved training program merely because the calendar slot ended.</p>
<h2 id="practice-agenda">A 60-minute sales practice agenda</h2>
<p>The example uses pairs practicing in parallel. Explain the roles and observation rules before starting. For a larger group, choose an appropriate facilitation plan and enough time for each participant; do not assume every rep can complete a long individual presentation inside the same hour.</p>
<div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Sixty-minute sales practice agenda" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Output: practice notes and an owned follow-through action</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Minutes</th><th scope="col">Activity</th><th scope="col">Evidence to capture</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">0–5 · 5 minutes</th><td>Facilitator states the single skill, explains the fictional scenario, and confirms participation needs.</td><td>Shared objective and roles.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">5–12 · 7 minutes</th><td>Facilitator demonstrates the question and restatement, then discusses what the buyer actually said.</td><td>One example linked to the observation rubric.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">12–22 · 10 minutes</th><td>Pairs run a first attempt: four minutes per learner and two minutes for brief notes.</td><td>Observed behavior and words, without inferred motives.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">22–30 · 8 minutes</th><td>Pairs discuss one useful behavior and one specific revision; facilitator resolves questions.</td><td>A defined change to try next.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">30–42 · 12 minutes</th><td>Pairs repeat the task: five minutes per learner and two minutes for notes.</td><td>A second observation using the same criteria.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">42–52 · 10 minutes</th><td>Group compares what changed, what stayed unclear, and which assumptions were unsupported.</td><td>Practice findings and unresolved questions.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">52–60 · 8 minutes</th><td>Learners choose a next practice action; manager confirms the review owner, evidence, and follow-up arrangement.</td><td>An owned action and a review plan.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The durations reconcile: 5 + 7 + 10 + 8 + 12 + 10 + 8 = 60 minutes. Time allocations are planning choices. If a discussion needs longer, agree what will move rather than silently removing the second practice or the follow-through decision.</p>
<h2 id="practice-prompt">A fictional discovery role-play</h2>
<p><strong>Buyer brief:</strong> a sales leader says reps use different evidence to move an opportunity into a qualified stage. Some rely on a meeting being booked; others wait for a scoped buyer problem. The buyer has not established the effect on conversion or revenue and has not approved a training purchase.</p>
<p><strong>Learner task:</strong> ask how the qualification decision happens today, listen, and restate the answer without adding an unverified consequence. Then identify what evidence would help the buyer decide whether training is relevant.</p>
<blockquote><p>Question: “What evidence does a rep currently need before moving an opportunity into the qualified stage?”</p><p>Fictional buyer response: “There isn't a common rule. Each rep uses their own judgment.”</p><p>Possible restatement: “The stage decision currently depends on each rep's judgment. Have I understood that correctly? What would your team need to agree before a shared rule would be useful?”</p></blockquote>
<p>This is one possible exchange. A buyer who says a shared rule already exists has changed the evidence. Respond to that answer instead of continuing a script that assumes a problem. Do not supply an invented cost, emotional motive, or urgent deadline to make the role-play sound persuasive.</p>
<h2 id="review">Review observable behavior</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Question:</strong> did the learner ask about the actual workflow without supplying the desired answer?</li><li><strong>Restatement:</strong> did the learner preserve the meaning of the response and invite correction?</li><li><strong>Next evidence:</strong> did the learner identify a relevant unanswered question before proposing a solution?</li></ul>
<p>For each criterion, record observed, not yet demonstrated, or not observed in this attempt, with a brief example. Keep “not observed” distinct from failure when the role-play did not provide an opportunity to demonstrate the behavior. Use the same criteria for both attempts and let the learner respond to the feedback.</p>
<p>A stronger second practice attempt is a practice observation. It does not prove that a program caused higher sales performance. Any later evaluation needs its own population, baseline, definitions, review period, and evidence. Attendance and a completed worksheet alone cannot establish a business result.</p>
